# Shell scripts must check out with LF even on Windows.
#
# core.autocrlf=true otherwise gives the Windows working tree CRLF copies. Git stores LF and the
# cloud hosts pull LF, so campaigns run correctly -- but only as long as deployment goes through
# git. Copying a working-tree script straight to a Linux driver ships the CRLF version, and bash
# then fails to parse it ("syntax error near unexpected token $'in\r'"). That happened once, to
# the two trial runners, while a campaign was live.
#
# Pinning eol=lf makes the working-tree copy identical to the stored one, so scp is safe too.
*.sh text eol=lf

# The Java client's pinned jar fingerprints, for the same reason one step removed. `sha256sum -c`
# takes the whole line as the file name, so a CRLF copy asks for "kafka-clients.jar\r", which does
# not exist: the check fails, the build stops, and the message names a fingerprint mismatch that
# never happened. LF here means the file reads the same in Git Bash on Windows as on the driver.
harness/java/DEPENDENCIES text eol=lf

# Same reasoning for the other files that are read by Linux tooling.
*.yaml text eol=lf
*.yml text eol=lf
Dockerfile* text eol=lf

# A plan freeze is checked against the SHA-256 fingerprints in its folder (freezes/README.md), so
# git must store and check out every file in it byte for byte, with no line-ending conversion.
freezes/*/** -text
